From 58e5854812263d02fa2b8f6a66ba28b884ddd03c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 04:38:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [TENT][ROCm] Register HipGemAllocator buffers through their
 dma-buf

A HipGemAllocator buffer is an amdgpu GEM BO imported into HIP. ROCr cannot
export it as a dma-buf and ibv_reg_mr must never see it (the amdgpu
peer-memory client dereferences the GEM BO's NULL kfd_bo), so TENT has
refused these buffers so far.

RocmPlatform::exportDmabuf now looks the buffer up in hip_gem_allocator.so
(mc_hip_gem_lookup) and returns a new descriptor of the BO's dma-buf and the
buffer's offset in it, so RdmaContext registers it with ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr.
Ordinary HIP memory keeps the default (NotImplemented, ibv_reg_mr).

HIP reports imported memory as device 0's whichever device imported it, so
RocmPlatform::getLocation() and getPointerDeviceId() take a GEM buffer's
device from the allocator too: the location picks the NICs on both ends of
a transfer.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
---
 docs/source/design/transfer-engine/index.md   |  2 +-
 .../tent/include/tent/platform/rocm.h         |  4 ++++
 .../tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_allocator.cpp | 21 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_probe.cpp     | 16 ++++++++++++--
 4 files changed, 40 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/source/design/transfer-engine/index.md b/docs/source/design/transfer-engine/index.md
index 24ab6db3..2de30a6b 100644
--- a/docs/source/design/transfer-engine/index.md
+++ b/docs/source/design/transfer-engine/index.md
@@ -530,7 +530,7 @@ For advanced users, TransferEngine provides the following advanced runtime optio
 - `MC_RDMA_DATA_DIRECT_PAIRING` Set to `1` (with `MC_RDMA_DATA_DIRECT=1`) to pair each GPU with the NICs whose Data Direct function shares the GPU's PCI root and to keep all other NICs out of that GPU's HCA list. Fail-closed: topology discovery is rejected when any NIC reports no Data Direct function or any GPU has no paired NIC, instead of falling back to PCIe distance (default: disabled).
 - `MC_HIP_STREAM_PRIORITY` ROCm only. `high` opts HIP transfer streams into the device's greatest supported stream priority; unset or `default` preserves normal priority. Set it before creating the first stream pool. Invalid values warn and retain the default. Streams remain nonblocking. Priority is a scheduling hint, not a preemption guarantee; measure copy latency and compute interference on your workload before enabling it.
 - `MC_HIP_KFD_PIN_FIX` ROCm only. amdgpu charges a NIC's dma-buf pin of HIP device memory to the free VRAM that `hipMemGetInfo` reports and new allocations draw from, so registering a large buffer can leave too little for kernel loads. With `1` (the default), the first HIP registration probes the driver by pinning one page of a private 64 MiB allocation through dma-buf, and if it miscounts, Mooncake holds one page-sized `ibv_reg_mr()` MR per registered allocation (needs the amdgpu peer-memory client) to keep free VRAM exact. The anchor only helps drivers whose KFD allocation limit credits peer-memory pins back, such as amdgpu-dkms 6.16. From amdgpu-dkms 6.18.4 through at least 7.1.9 the limit subtracts every pin, through dma-buf or the peer-memory client alike, so the anchor corrects what `hipMemGetInfo` reports but not what allocations get; use `mooncake.allocator.HipGemAllocator` (below) for the registered buffers there. The driver fix is torvalds/linux 9131cc85d0de ("drm/amdkfd: correct vram_available calculation", v7.4-rc1). `force` holds them without probing; `0` disables the workaround. Cost: each registered HIP allocation takes one extra MR, always on the first NIC that accepts it rather than spread across NICs, until its last registered buffer is released. A few large KV allocations add a few entries, but many distinct `hipMalloc` allocations can fill a small MR table (for example on Ionic NICs); once that `ibv_reg_mr()` fails, anchors are turned off for the process and the double charge returns.
-- `MC_HIP_GEM_PIN_NIC` ROCm only. The RDMA device `mooncake.allocator.HipGemAllocator` pins each new buffer through (default: the first one that accepts a dma-buf MR). That allocator (`hip_gem_allocator.so`, a `torch.cuda.memory.CUDAPluggableAllocator` backend for a `torch.cuda.MemPool`) is for HIP buffers a NIC will pin, such as a KV cache. On amdgpu-dkms 6.18.4 through at least 7.1.9, and on kernels without torvalds/linux 9131cc85d0de, the KFD allocation limit counts a pinned `hipMalloc` buffer twice (once allocated, once pinned): registering a KV cache larger than the VRAM left after allocating it makes every later device allocation of the process fail, the first new HIP stream typically. Each `HipGemAllocator` buffer is an amdgpu GEM BO imported into HIP; it is charged once, and pinned at allocation by a one-page dma-buf MR so `hipMemGetInfo` covers it from the start. The transfer engine registers such buffers through the BO's dma-buf whatever `MOONCAKE_DISABLE_HIP_DMABUF` says (so the kernel must support dma-buf MRs), never through `ibv_reg_mr()`, and leaves them out of the HIP transport, which has no IPC handle for them: same-host transfers to them go over RDMA. HIP reports imported memory as device 0's, so the transfer engine takes their GPU from the allocator: their location, and with it the NICs picked on both ends of a transfer, follows the GPU they were allocated on. TENT (`MC_USE_TENT`) cannot register them yet: it refuses them rather than passing them to `ibv_reg_mr()`, so use this allocator with the classic transfer engine.
+- `MC_HIP_GEM_PIN_NIC` ROCm only. The RDMA device `mooncake.allocator.HipGemAllocator` pins each new buffer through (default: the first one that accepts a dma-buf MR). That allocator (`hip_gem_allocator.so`, a `torch.cuda.memory.CUDAPluggableAllocator` backend for a `torch.cuda.MemPool`) is for HIP buffers a NIC will pin, such as a KV cache. On amdgpu-dkms 6.18.4 through at least 7.1.9, and on kernels without torvalds/linux 9131cc85d0de, the KFD allocation limit counts a pinned `hipMalloc` buffer twice (once allocated, once pinned): registering a KV cache larger than the VRAM left after allocating it makes every later device allocation of the process fail, the first new HIP stream typically. Each `HipGemAllocator` buffer is an amdgpu GEM BO imported into HIP; it is charged once, and pinned at allocation by a one-page dma-buf MR so `hipMemGetInfo` covers it from the start. The transfer engine registers such buffers through the BO's dma-buf whatever `MOONCAKE_DISABLE_HIP_DMABUF` says (so the kernel must support dma-buf MRs), never through `ibv_reg_mr()`, and leaves them out of the HIP transport, which has no IPC handle for them: same-host transfers to them go over RDMA. HIP reports imported memory as device 0's, so the transfer engine takes their GPU from the allocator: their location, and with it the NICs picked on both ends of a transfer, follows the GPU they were allocated on. TENT registers them through the same dma-buf, takes their GPU from the allocator too, and skips its `ibv_reg_mr()` warm-up for them.
 - `MC_ENDPOINT_STORE_TYPE` Choose FIFO Endpoint Store (`FIFO`) or Sieve Endpoint Store (`SIEVE`), default is `SIEVE`.
 - `MC_TCP_ENABLE_CONNECTION_POOL` Reuse TCP connections across transfers to the same peer. Enabled by default: instead of opening a fresh socket per transfer, work for one peer runs over a bounded set of connection lanes (`MC_TCP_LANES_PER_PEER`, default 4), which keeps high-concurrency TCP traffic from exhausting the initiator's ephemeral port range. A lane's socket is reused only after a cleanly completed exchange; a connection whose protocol stream went dirty is closed and re-established. Set to `0` (or `false`/`no`) to restore one connection per transfer.
 - `MC_TCP_MAX_QUEUED_BYTES_PER_PEER` Optional byte limit for work waiting in each TCP connection group's transfer queue or pending admission. Active lanes are excluded because their count is already bounded by `MC_TCP_LANES_PER_PEER`. Endpoint refresh may keep retiring groups alive alongside the current group, so this is not a single aggregate limit across endpoint generations. Exceeding the limit fails the new transfer with `QUEUE_FULL`; confirm the caller's retry and failure behavior before enabling it, because frameworks may escalate this terminal transfer failure to a request or peer-session failure. Admission is per transfer item rather than atomic across a multi-request batch: a later rejection does not roll back earlier admitted items. Callers that use synchronous batch APIs must not treat a nonzero return as proof that no destination bytes changed or that every source buffer is immediately reusable. Unset or `0` disables the byte limit.
diff --git a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/include/tent/platform/rocm.h b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/include/tent/platform/rocm.h
index 3c1d4e82..e8808f31 100644
--- a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/include/tent/platform/rocm.h
+++ b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/include/tent/platform/rocm.h
@@ -119,6 +119,10 @@ class RocmPlatform : public Platform {
 
     Status synchronizeDevices(const Topology* topology) override;
 
+    // Only hip_gem_allocator.so buffers (rocm_gem.h): ordinary HIP memory
+    // keeps the default (NotImplemented, registered with ibv_reg_mr).
+    Status exportDmabuf(void* addr, size_t length, DmabufExport& out) override;
+
     Status getStreamFromPool(HIPStreamHandle& outHandle,
                              int deviceId = HIPStreamPool::kCurrentDevice);
 
diff --git a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_allocator.cpp b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_allocator.cpp
index da53cee9..2fc233d7 100644
--- a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_allocator.cpp
+++ b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_allocator.cpp
@@ -13,16 +13,37 @@
 // limitations under the License.
 
 #include "tent/platform/rocm.h"
+#include "tent/platform/rocm_gem.h"
 #include "tent/common/status.h"
 
 #include <hip/hip_runtime.h>
 #include <numa.h>
 #include <glog/logging.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 #include <vector>
 
 namespace mooncake {
 namespace tent {
 
+Status RocmPlatform::exportDmabuf(void* addr, size_t length,
+                                  DmabufExport& out) {
+    out = DmabufExport{};
+    void* base = nullptr;
+    size_t size = 0;
+    int fd = -1;
+    if (!lookupHipGemBuffer(addr, &base, &size, &fd, nullptr))
+        return Platform::exportDmabuf(addr, length, out);
+    if ((uintptr_t)addr + length > (uintptr_t)base + size) {
+        ::close(fd);
+        return Status::InvalidArgument(
+            "buffer runs past its amdgpu GEM allocation" LOC_MARK);
+    }
+    out.fd = fd;
+    out.offset = (uintptr_t)addr - (uintptr_t)base;
+    out.close_fd = true;
+    return Status::OK();
+}
+
 Status RocmPlatform::allocate(void** pptr, size_t size,
                               MemoryOptions& options) {
     LocationParser location(options.location);
diff --git a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_probe.cpp b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_probe.cpp
index 50f6de1b..6e1fbf5e 100644
--- a/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_probe.cpp
+++ b/mooncake-transfer-engine/tent/src/platform/rocm/rocm_probe.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 // limitations under the License.
 
 #include "tent/platform/rocm.h"
+#include "tent/platform/rocm_gem.h"
 #include "tent/common/status.h"
 #include "tent/common/utils/prefault.h"
 #include "char_util.h"
@@ -263,6 +264,16 @@ MemoryType RocmPlatform::getMemoryType(void* addr) {
     return MTYPE_CPU;
 }
 
+// HIP reports memory imported with hipImportExternalMemory, such as a
+// HipGemAllocator buffer, as device 0's whichever device imported it; the
+// allocator knows the device it was allocated on.
+static int deviceOfPointer(void* addr, int reported_device) {
+    int device = -1;
+    return lookupHipGemBuffer(addr, nullptr, nullptr, nullptr, &device)
+               ? device
+               : reported_device;
+}
+
 int RocmPlatform::getPointerDeviceId(void* addr) {
     hipPointerAttribute_t attributes{};
     if (hipPointerGetAttributes(&attributes, addr) != hipSuccess) {
@@ -274,7 +285,7 @@ int RocmPlatform::getPointerDeviceId(void* addr) {
     if (attributes.type != hipMemoryTypeDevice) {
         return HIPStreamPool::kCurrentDevice;
     }
-    return attributes.device;
+    return deviceOfPointer(addr, attributes.device);
 }
 
 static inline uintptr_t alignPage(uintptr_t address) {
@@ -309,7 +320,8 @@ const std::vector<RangeLocation> RocmPlatform::getLocation(void* start,
 
     if (attributes.type == hipMemoryTypeDevice) {
         entries.push_back(
-            {(uint64_t)start, len, genRocmNodeName(attributes.device)});
+            {(uint64_t)start, len,
+             genRocmNodeName(deviceOfPointer(start, attributes.device))});
         return entries;
     }
 
